Rule 1 (If the 'other' argument in torch.rsub is a tensor, its dtype must match the input tensor's dtype.)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= dtype_(v_1) = dtype_(v_2)
>>
Rule 2 (If the 'other' argument in torch.rsub is a tensor, both tensors must have at least one dimension.)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) > 0 ∧ ndim(v_2) > 0
>>
Rule 3 (If 'other' is a tensor and alpha is provided, the resulting tensor's dtype is the same as the input tensors when alpha is 1.)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor, v_3 : int ⊎ float} |= if v_3 = 1 then dtype_(v_1) = dtype_(v_2)
>>
Rule 4 (If 'other' is a tensor, and its shape is different from the input tensor, some broadcasting is necessary.)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if ndim(v_1) ≠ ndim(v_2) then true else ∃i ∈ [0, ndim(v_1) - 1] : shape(v_1, i) ≠ shape(v_2, i)
>>
Rule 5 (If 'other' is a boolean tensor, then the input tensor must also be a boolean tensor.)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if dtype_(v_2) = 0 then dtype_(v_1) = 0
>>
Rule 6 (If a tensor is rsubed by an integer and the input tensor is of boolean type, the operation is equivalent to logical negation.)
{v_1 : tensor} |= if dtype_(v_1) = 0 then true
